# {{ if .DisplayName }}{{ .DisplayName }}{{ else }}{{ .Container }}{{ end }}

The `{{ .Container }}` service of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}, in project {{ .Project }}: an HTTP
server in TypeScript on EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group, behind an
internal Application Load Balancer, keeping items in a DynamoDB table, with
its infrastructure as a CDK app. Scaffolded by C4 Factory.

## The API

The load balancer is internal: it answers only from inside the service's
VPC, over HTTPS on port 443, with the ACM certificate a deploy names in the
stack's `CertificateArn` parameter; there is no plain HTTP.

| Route             | Body                                | Answers                                    |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| `PUT /items/{id}` | `{ "name": "Lamp", "quantity": 3 }` | 200 and the item; 400 for a bad id or body |
| `GET /items/{id}` |                                     | 200 and the item; 404 when there is none   |
| `GET /health`     |                                     | 200 while the server is up                 |

An id is 1 to 64 letters, digits, `-` or `_`. A name is 1 to 200 characters;
a quantity a whole number, 0 or more. A body is at most 64 KiB (413 past it).
Every error is JSON with a `message`. A `PUT` replaces the whole item, so
repeating it is harmless.

## Develop

Open the repository in its devcontainer, which has Node 24 and the Go toolchain.

```sh
npm ci           # install the exact packages in package-lock.json
npm test         # unit tests and the stacks' assertions, with vitest
npm run synth    # write the CloudFormation templates into cdk.out/
{{- if .Standard }}
make check       # what CI runs: format, lint, type check, test, synth, cfn-lint
make help        # every target
{{- end }}
```

`TABLE_NAME=items npm start` runs the server on port 8080 (`PORT` changes
it); it needs AWS credentials for a real table.

Nothing here deploys or needs an AWS account: synth runs offline, bundles the
server with the installed esbuild, and needs neither Docker nor credentials.

## Tiers and operations

The stacks are built for a tier, `production` or `development`, which is the
place they run, not the code: `make synth` writes both, into
`cdk.out/production/` and `cdk.out/development/`, and a deploy picks one
(`cdk deploy -c tier=production`; without `-c tier` it is `development`).
The production stack is `{{ .Container }}`, the development one
`{{ .Container }}-development`, so a deploy that names no tier never changes
production.
Production keeps the table when the stack goes, protects it and the stack
from deletion, recovers it to any second of the last 35 days, backs it up
daily with AWS Backup and keeps logs a year; development throws its data
away with the stack and keeps logs a month. Both tiers alarm and have a
dashboard; neither traces. The instances hold no data, so their disks are
never backed up.

What the app promises whatever the tier, the backups' retention and copy
region, the recovery objectives and who the alarms tell, is in
`infra/operations.ts`, which C4 Factory renders from the Container's
`spec.operations`: change the block, and the file follows it. A deploy sets
two parameters per environment: `CertificateArn`, the load balancer's
certificate, which it needs, and `AlertEmail`, who the alarms tell. Each
stack outputs `EntryPoint`, the load balancer's DNS name, and
`AlarmTopicArn`, the topic its alarms and the group's scaling events
notify.

Deploying (`cdk deploy -c tier=production --parameters CertificateArn=<arn>`,
after `cdk bootstrap` of the account) is for its owner, with their own
credentials.

## How it runs

Each instance runs Amazon Linux 2023 on Graviton (`t4g.small`). At its first
boot it installs Node.js 22, the CloudWatch agent and `cfn-signal` from the
Amazon Linux repositories, downloads the bundled server (`server.mjs`) from
the CDK asset bucket, and runs the server under systemd as its own user; the
agent ships the server's log to CloudWatch Logs. Once the server answers
`GET /health`, the instance signals CloudFormation that it is ready. The
instances sit in isolated subnets in two zones with no route to the
internet: they reach S3, DynamoDB, CloudWatch Logs and CloudFormation
through VPC endpoints. The group keeps two to four instances, scales on
CPU, and replaces one that fails the load balancer's `GET /health` check.
On a change it replaces them one at a time, starting each new one before
stopping an old one and waiting for its signal: when a new instance fails
to come up healthy, the update stops and CloudFormation rolls back.

## Layout

- `src/main.ts` is the entry point the instances run: it reads `TABLE_NAME`
  and `PORT`, creates the DynamoDB client once and starts the server; on
  `SIGTERM` it finishes the requests in flight.
- `src/server.ts` reads each HTTP request and hands it to `answer` in
  `src/api.ts`, which routes it, checks its input and answers with a status
  code; `src/items.ts` holds the rules, free of AWS; `src/item_store.ts`
  keeps items in DynamoDB behind the `ItemStore` interface tests fake.
- `infra/stacks.ts` defines the stacks for a tier; `infra/service_stack.ts`
  is the main one: the table, the VPC and its endpoints, the instances' role,
  launch template and Auto Scaling group, the load balancer with its access
  log bucket, the alarms (`infra/alarms.ts`) and, in production, the backups
  (`infra/backups.ts`). `infra/server_asset.ts` bundles the server for it.
  The stack is named after the package. `infra/operations.ts` holds what the
  Container promises.
{{- if .Standard }}
- `infra/main.ts` is the CDK app `cdk synth` runs (`cdk.json`), and
  `infra/standard.ts` says what each tier means, tags every stack and adds
  CDK Nag's checks. `docs/runbook.md` says how to restore the data, roll a
  deployment back, and what to do when an alarm fires.

Read `AGENTS.md` before changing anything: it is the rulebook, for people and agents.
{{- end }}
